{
  // [P6.8.7a-1] — the 17 house gates had NO typecheck in any live gate, and that is the THIRD time this
  // exact hole has been found in this repo. `apps/rn/tsconfig.json` records closing it for the RN app;
  // `packages/core/tsconfig.json` records closing it for the money engine ("the LEAST type-checked code
  // in the repo") and, in the same breath, notes that "the root tsconfig is broken and referenced by no
  // script" — which is precisely why `scripts/**` fell through: it is included ONLY by that dead config.
  //
  // ⛔ The hole was about to become invisible rather than merely open: the root tsconfig belongs to the
  // legacy Next surface that P6.11 DELETES, so after that commit nothing would have included these files
  // at all and nothing would have reported the change.
  //
  // ⚠️ What made it worth closing now rather than filing: these scripts ARE the gates. `lint:money`,
  // `lint:closure`, `lint:sandbox`, the fingerprint that decides whether a recorded pass still describes
  // the tree — all of it ran with no compiler behind it, while every line of the code they guard had one.
  //
  // Measured before wiring it up: 3 errors across 26 scripts, all trivial, none in gate logic.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    // `import.meta.dirname` is how every script here locates the repo root; it needs ES modules and the
    // node types, not the DOM.
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    // These run under `tsx`, which resolves both spellings — `./gateSources` and `./coverage-model.ts`
    // are both live in the tree. `bundler` covers the extensionless form; this covers the explicit one.
    // Requires `noEmit`, which is what a typecheck-only config wants anyway.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "types": ["node"],
    // ⛔ **S1.10.6.9 — `npm run typecheck` WAS RED ON THE COMMITTED TREE AND NOTHING SAID SO.** [`G-6`]
    //
    // ⚡ A gate that imports app source drags that source into THIS program, where the app's path aliases
    // do not exist — so `check-trust-claims.ts`'s import of `trustSelectors.ts` (added the day before, by
    // the fix for `C-1`) turned every `@/…` and `@core/…` in the reachable graph into `TS2307`, and every
    // implicitly-typed parameter behind them into `TS7006`: **18 errors, none of them real.**
    //
    // ⛔ **`typecheck` is `core && rn && scripts && tests`, so the failure took `typecheck:tests` with it**
    // — the suite never ran at all — and `validate:release:rn` starts with `npm run typecheck`, which means
    // the release gate could not have passed. ⚠️ Every per-fix *"typecheck green"* recorded since was
    // `typecheck:rn` alone. That is this cluster's own defect class — an instrument reporting less than it
    // claims — living in the chain the fixes are VERIFIED with rather than in the code being fixed.
    //
    // ⚠️ Aliases, not an exclusion. Telling this config to skip the app would restore the green by making
    // the gates' own imports unchecked, which is the hole `[P6.8.7a-1]` above closed.
    "baseUrl": "..",
    "paths": {
      "@core/*": ["packages/core/*"],
      "@/*": ["apps/rn/src/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules"]
}
